course: dictionary
lesson: 1993
title: Compute: work it out
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: «Compute» is a formal word for work out, usually with numbers. «Compute the total cost of the shopping» means add up all the prices to find the final amount. The words after it, such as total and cost, tell you what kind of working out is needed.
explain: The word is hiding inside «computer». A computer is a machine that can compute, which means it can work out answers very quickly. When you see «compute» in a maths question, you are being asked to calculate, not to guess.
explain: Grown-ups sometimes use it as a joke. In a robot voice, someone might say «It does not compute», which means something makes no sense. Context decides which meaning is right: a maths question means calculate, but a silly robot voice means it is confusing.
fact: To compute means to work out an answer by calculating.
fact: «Compute» is a formal word. In everyday talk people say «work out», «add up», «do the maths» or «crunch the numbers».
fact: «It does not compute» is a joke way to say something makes no sense.
fact: In the 1940s six women programmed ENIAC, one of the first electronic computers.
fact: «Grace Hopper was a computing pioneer»: she was one of the first people to help build the field of computing.
exercise: «Compute the total cost of the three books.» What does compute mean here? | Throw away | *Work out by calculating | Read the title of | Draw a picture of
exercise: Sam says «It does not compute» when his teacher explains a riddle that makes no sense. What does he mean? | It is a very big number | Ask me again tomorrow | It is correct and finished | *It makes no sense
exercise: «Compute the area of the square.» Which everyday words could you use instead of compute? | Go to sleep | Read a poem | Wave hello | *Do the maths
test: «Compute the average of 4, 6 and 8.» What does this command ask you to do? | Put the numbers in alphabetical order | Draw three stars | Say the numbers aloud | *Find the answer by calculating
test: In «Routes into computing include university», what does computing mean? | Counting sheep | Going shopping for new computers | Singing in a choir | *Working with computers and writing programs
exam: «Compute the total expenditure incurred.» What is the best plain-English meaning? | *Work out the total amount spent | Write a long poem about money | Count the tiny pieces of the budget | Spend the money as quickly as you can
